WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.740
When Android engineers transition to react native, they encounter a different API surface for navigation.

2
00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:10.960
While translating routes and managing stacks may look unfamiliar,

3
00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:14.320
the architectural goals of both platforms are aligned.

4
00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:22.120
The underlying declarative routing philosophy between Jetpack Compose and React Navigation 7 follows the same core logic.

5
00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:29.560
These systems share three pillars, a navigator container, a composable route graph, and backstack management.

6
00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:36.360
Both use typed routes to catch mismatches during builds, support nested architectures for isolated tabs,

7
00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:40.520
and rely on programmatic hooks to maneuver users between nodes.

8
00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:48.840
The divergence lies in the specific execution, how each system defines route typing, and how they model their respective backstack states.

9
00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:56.440
With this shared foundation established, the task for the Android engineer is to map their existing mental models to this new syntax.

10
00:00:56.440 --> 00:01:00.360
In Compose, the standard pattern utilizes a single nav host.

11
00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:06.600
This entity manages both the controller provider and the composable graph scope in one block.

12
00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:12.040
React Navigation 7 splits that responsibility across two distinct components.

13
00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:17.600
The first is navigation container, hosting the controller provider and managing state.

14
00:01:17.600 --> 00:01:23.960
The second is stack.navigator, the composable graph scope containing individual route definitions.

15
00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:29.080
Once the container and graph scope are established, we define the screens within that graph.

16
00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:36.040
The familiar Compose Composable function translates directly into the React Navigation stack.screen component.

17
00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:43.000
By separating the container from the graph, React Navigation provides a modular baseline that mimics native Android architecture,

18
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:45.640
while fitting into React's component tree.

19
00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:53.920
Moving data between these screens requires rigid type safety to ensure that the parameters sent by one screen match what the destination expects to receive.

20
00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:58.640
Jetpack Compose handles this natively by utilizing Kotlin object-based routes.

21
00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:04.880
React Navigation 7 achieves the same result using TypeScript Param types to enforce these contracts.

22
00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:09.840
The core of this type safety is the TypeScript interface native stack screen props.

23
00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:15.520
React screen components ingest this interface, defining exactly which parameters they can receive.

24
00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:20.000
Validated by the compiler, you access them with the same confidence as Kotlin.

25
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:23.760
The mechanism shifts from Kotlin objects to TypeScript interfaces.

26
00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:28.000
But the result, strict type compliance for the application, is the same.

27
00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:34.240
To trigger these transitions, Compose's nav controller translates to React Navigation's Use Navigation Hook.

28
00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:37.920
We can map the most common operations directly from one to the other.

29
00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:43.040
Forward Navigation using NavigateRoot is identical to calling Navigation.Navigate,

30
00:02:43.040 --> 00:02:47.760
reversing the stack with popback stack translates to Navigation.GoBack.

31
00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:51.840
Up Navigation using NavigateUp also maps directly to GoBack.

32
00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:55.600
For destructive actions like clearing the stack with popup2,

33
00:02:55.600 --> 00:03:00.160
React Navigation uses Navigation.Reset or pop to top.

34
00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:05.760
Finally, inspecting the current state maps to Navigation.State or UseRoute.

35
00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:10.240
The hook-based API covers the same programmatic surface area as Compose,

36
00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:12.800
but with more consolidated method names.

37
00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:18.480
Simple transitions are easy to map, but complex flows like authentication require a different approach

38
00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:20.720
to keep the user in the correct state.

39
00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:26.400
An imperative approach, manually firing popback stack multiple times to clear login screens,

40
00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:29.280
is brittle and often leads to race conditions.

41
00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:33.360
Modern declarative UI discourages this manual stack manipulation

42
00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:38.080
because it relies on the developer to perfectly track the stack's history at all times.

43
00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:43.280
Instead, we use a declarative paradigm where global state dictates which part of the navigation

44
00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:48.080
graph exists. We define a global Boolean toggle for the authentication state.

45
00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:53.520
When false, the system renders an isolated auth graph containing login screens.

46
00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:58.480
In Compose, you'd use a standard if block within Navhost to switch composable trees.

47
00:03:59.040 --> 00:04:03.200
React Navigation conditionally renders different stack.navigator components

48
00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:06.480
based on that same state. When the state becomes true,

49
00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:09.680
the auth graph is destroyed and replaced by the main graph.

50
00:04:09.680 --> 00:04:12.960
Because the login screens no longer exist in the component tree,

51
00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:15.920
the hardware back button cannot return the user to them.

52
00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:20.320
Success in React navigation requires moving away from manual pop actions

53
00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:22.960
and letting conditional rendering manage the stack's existence.

54
00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:28.480
When we zoom out, we can see how these modular blocks build a production app architecture.

55
00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:32.560
A common pattern is to place a tab.navigator at the root of the application.

56
00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:36.800
Individual tabs then encapsulate their own nested stack.navigator trees.

57
00:04:36.800 --> 00:04:40.160
React navigation supports this hierarchical nesting natively,

58
00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:43.440
keeping the history of each tab isolated just as composed as.

59
00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:49.040
Beyond internal navigation, we also have to account for external entry points.

60
00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:54.400
Deep linking configuration in React navigation maps directly to native OS routing.

61
00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:58.480
On Android, you still register intent filters in the Android manifest

62
00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:03.440
to route URIs into the React container, which then targets the correct node in your graph.

63
00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:07.120
By combining these nested stacks with native deep linking,

64
00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:11.120
React navigation achieves the same feature set as Jetpack Compose.

65
00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:14.400
As we've seen, the mental models you've built in Android development

66
00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:16.720
carry over directly to React native.

67
00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:21.520
Success is a matter of mapping those concepts to TypeScript and these new component containers.

68
00:05:21.520 --> 00:05:24.000
Now that we've mapped the behavior of the application,

69
00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:25.600
we can look at its appearance.

70
00:05:25.600 --> 00:05:28.160
In our final video, we'll translate material three

71
00:05:28.160 --> 00:05:37.040
theming patterns into React native styling systems.

